FEATURE: resizer-example
DATE: 2026-08-27
SCOPE: runnable example page for the Resizer module
FOLLOWS: changelog/resizer-fixes

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
AFFECTED PROJECTS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

vUX library (this repository) — the only project affected.

  - examples/resizer/index.html   new, five sections on the shared design system
  - examples/resizer/main.js      new, commented as documentation
  - examples/resizer/README.md    new
  - vUX-resizer.js                one fix surfaced by building the page
  - ChangeLog.md                  two bullets under v4.0.0-beta

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHY
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

CLAUDE.md requires a runnable example of every module. Resizer had none — it was one
of two repaired modules in that state, and the omission had a direct cost: the
preceding resizer-fixes work could only be verified statically, because there was no
page from which to drive a real drag or to confirm that resizer.css resolves at
runtime under a live data-library-root.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
THE PAGE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  Hero              one box resizable on both axes, clamped 220x120 to 980x520,
                    live size readout driven by config.callBack
  01 Playground     every config property as a live control, an equivalent-code
                    panel, and resizedWidth/resizedHeight/callback-count as they move
  02 Your own       config.myResizeHandler driving a split view from a grip that is a
     handle         sibling of the resized panel and contains an icon
  03 All four       position.x left/right and position.y top/bottom, each growing in
     edges          the direction the handle faces
  04 Teardown       destroy() / initialize(), with a live count of handles in the DOM
  05 Bad input      thirteen deliberately-bad calls, printing the real thrown Error

Section choices track what resizer-fixes changed, so the page doubles as that work's
demonstration. Section 03 exists for the left/top inversion, which is new behaviour.
Section 02 exercises an option that was previously stored and used for nothing, with
a handle shaped to exercise both of the binding defects (a sibling rather than a
child; an icon inside it). Section 04 exists because destroy() is new. Two of the
validation cases — an empty resizeHandlerProperties object and an unknown key — cover
a bare TypeError and a silent no-op respectively.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
ONE FIX FOUND WHILE BUILDING IT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The playground rebuilds its Resizer on every control change, which made a leak
obvious within a dozen clicks: attachStyleSheet() appends a <style> unconditionally,
so every rebuild that configured a handle style left the previous one in <head> for
ever. Invisible before destroy() existed, because initialize() was called once and
never again. buildHandler() now removes the element carrying the same data-id before
attaching. See logs/vUX-resizer.js_ai_change.log.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
CONSUMER-SIDE TECHNIQUES THE PAGE DOCUMENTS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Three things the library deliberately does not do, each of which a consumer will
otherwise hit and misread as the component being broken. All three are commented in
the page and explained in its README.

  1. Hit area. resizer.css gives a handle everything structural but no resting
     appearance, and 2px is a hard target for a mouse. The page separates the strip
     you can grab (14px) from the strip you can see (3px).
  2. Specificity and source order. resizer.css is injected during initialize(), so it
     lands after the page's own <style> block; a rule written as bare .resizeHandle
     ties and loses. Page rules are scoped to a .rz class. A rule generated from
     config styles is later still, so config keeps the last word — which is what the
     playground's colour picker relies on.
  3. Anchoring. The module writes a size, never a position. Without anchoring the box
     to the far side of its container, a left- or top-handled element still resizes
     but visibly grows the opposite way, which reads as the inversion being broken.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
LIMITATION RECORDED RATHER THAN SOLVED
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Handles are matched by class name globally, so a style pushed through
config.resizeHandlerProperties.styles generates rules against .resizeHandle.x /
.resizeHandle.y and reaches every Resizer on the page. The example therefore styles
most of its boxes from its own CSS and uses the config route only in the playground.
The README states this and gives the workaround: a distinct handle class per instance
via config.myResizeHandler.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
VERIFICATION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

No browser is available in this environment — no chromium, no playwright, no
puppeteer — so the page has NOT been opened, and no drag has been performed. What was
checked:

  - index.html tag balance: section, style, script, pre, aside, main, footer, header
    and div all balanced (72 open / 72 close on div).
  - Every id referenced by main.js exists in index.html, and every id in index.html is
    either used by main.js or is a documented markup hook.
  - All 13 data-err buttons have a matching case, and all 13 cases have a button.
  - main.js parses as an ES module (it reaches a runtime `document is not defined`,
    which is exactly as far as it can get without a DOM).
  - vUX-resizer.js still parses after the stylesheet fix.

STILL UNVERIFIED, and the reason to open the page before trusting it:

  - real mouse-drag behaviour on any of the eight Resizer instances
  - that resizer.css resolves at runtime under data-library-root="../../"
  - the specificity claims in the README, which depend on injection order at runtime
  - responsive behaviour at the two breakpoints (900px, 620px)

Run `python3 -m http.server 8000` from the repository root and open
/examples/resizer/index.html to close all four.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
FOLLOW-UP
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Resizer still has no guide under doc/. doc/README.md lists it among the modules whose
guides are "coming next", and it is now the module with the most recently changed
behaviour — the left/top inversion and destroy() are both undocumented outside these
logs and the example's README.
